 "title": "Amino acids differ only in the R group",
 "caption": "Every amino acid has the same backbone, an amino group and a carboxyl group on the α-carbon, shaded here. Only the R group changes, and it decides how the residue behaves in a protein. Proteins use the L form (S in all of these but cysteine, where sulfur changes the priorities).",
 "alt": "Skeletal structures of glycine, alanine, serine, aspartic acid, lysine and cysteine, each with the amino group, alpha carbon and carboxyl group shaded.",
 "description": [
  "Glycine: R is a hydrogen atom, the simplest amino acid, and not chiral.",
  "Alanine: a methyl group, non-polar.",
  "Serine: a hydroxyl group, polar but uncharged.",
  "Aspartic acid: a second carboxyl group, acidic.",
  "Lysine: a second amino group, basic.",
  "Cysteine: a thiol (–SH) group, able to form disulfide bridges."
